   drop three times daily and Bion tears one     in complete sentences\nPlan                                      drop 4 hourly. A review was schedule after    Signal words continue time line\nChloramphenicol 0.5% sterile 1 drop TID   2 weeks.\nBion Tears 1 drop each eye 4 hrly                                                           Two weeks later,\nReview 2 weeks                                                                              In addition,\n                                                                                            Therefore,\n\f\n05.06.10                                    Unfortunately, today she was\n Subjective                                  accompanied by her husband with                   Summarises medical condition\n Accompanied by husband. Very                complaints of impaired vision in both             clearly and concisely with the\n distressed. Has lost most sight in both     eyes and an inability to read books or            expression \"vision Impairment\"\n eyes –can make out light or dark shapes     watch television. There was oedema in\n but unable to read or watch TV.             both eyelids with white discharge. She            Supporting sentences expand\n Objective                                   could not read the eye                            case notes into formal sentences\n Marked oedema upper and lower lids                                                            Signal words add sympathy\n White sticky discharge Unable to read\n eye chart                                                                                         Unfortunately,\n Plan\n Refer immediately Emergency Dept,\n Royal Melbourne Eye Hospital.\n Husband will drive to hospital\n\n\n\n\n   wink Handy Hint\nWhen describing the history, base your letter from today’s perspective. That means, include all the relevant past history, but in\nsummary form, eliminating less important detail and focus your attention on the current situation which is usually more\nimportant.\n\n\n\n\nLast modiﬁed: Sunday, 11 February 2018, 12:23 PM\n\n\n                                                       Return to: Topic 1 \n\f\nDoctors: Writing Course\n\n\n\n                                                         Conclusions\n\nThe conclusion or ﬁnal paragraph in the letter should be fairly standard in structure. It should be based on the task question which\nis found at the end of case notes. It may contain one or two of the following points:\n\n    a polite request of action required\n    a summary of your (suspected) diagnosis\n    a thank you for ongoing support\n    an offer of future assistance if required (this can be useful if you choose to omit some details from the case notes)\n\nIt is useful to be familiar with some standard patterns so that you are able to conclude your letter conﬁdently, quickly and most\nimportantly, accurately. However, some degree of originality will impress the assessors. Therefore, where possible try to ensure\nthat your conclusion is related to your task and not simply a memorised ending.\n     wink Handy hint\nWhen describing the chief complaint or your diagnosis, try to use different words or sentence structure to what you have said in\nthe introduction.\n\n\n                                                 Important Grammar Rules\n\nModal Verbs : Some modal verbs & the verb hope are used to convey politeness and commonly used for polite requests in the\nconclusion of formal letters. Compare the patterns below.\n\n                 Informal                                     Polite                                    More polite\n\f\nI want you to see him as early as           Please see him as early as                   I would appreciate it if you could\n     possible and advise him on                  possible and advise him on further           see him as early as possible and\n     further management.                         management.(this sounds like an              advise him on further\n     I will be pleased if you can                order so should not be used)                 management.\n     examine, diagnose and treat the             Please examine, diagnose and                 It would be greatly appreciated if\n     patient as you feel appropriate.            treat the patient as you feel                you could see him as early as\n     Can you take over her care for              appropriate.                                 possible and advise him on further\n     appropriate treatment.                      Please take over her care for                management. (passive and most\n                                                 appropriate treatment.                       polite)\n                                                                                              I would be grateful if you could\n                                                                                              examine, diagnose and treat the\n                                                                                              patient as you feel appropriate.\n                                                                                              (active)\n Explanation: These sentences are ﬁne in                                                      I would appreciate it if you could\n spoken English, but not suitable for                                                         examine, diagnose and treat the\n formal writing. Can and will are            Explanation: These sentences are                 patient as you feel appropriate.\n considered less polite than could and       acceptable in written English, but can           (active)\n would.                                      sound quite direct.                              I would appreciate it if you could\n                                                                                              take over her care for appropriate\n                                                                                              treatment.\n                                                                                              I would be grateful if you could\n                                                                                              take over her care for appropriate\n                                                                                              treatment.\n\n                                                                                          Explanation: These sentences are the\n                                                                                          most suitable for concluding requests in\n                                                                                          formal letters.\n\n\nConditional Sentences: These sentences are also frequently used in the conclusion of a referral letter and the rules are as follows:\n\n   Use a comma when the if clause is at the beginning of the          Don’t use a comma when the if clause is at the end of the\n                        sentence.                                                          sentence.\n\n\n     If you could take over her on going care,it would be               It would be greatly appreciated if you could take over\n     greatly appreciated.                                               her ongoing care.\n     If you have any further questions regarding this patient,          Please don’t hesitate to call me if you have any further\n     please don’t hesitate to call me.                                  questions regarding this patient.\n\n\n\n     If you require any more information, please don't                  Please don't hesitate to contact me if you require any\n     hesitate to contact me.(active)                                    more information.(active)\n     If any more information is required, please don't hesitate         Please don't hesitate to contact me if any more\n     to contact me.(passive)                                            information is required.(passive)\n\f\nShould you have any further queries, please don't                     Please don't hesitate to contact me should you have any\n     hesitate to contact me.                                               further queries.\n\n Note: Sometimes if is omitted from a conditional sentence. In\n full the sentence means:\n If you should have any further questions regarding this patient,\n please don’t hesitate to call me.\n\n\nCloser and signature\nLeave a space between the last line of the conclusion and the closer. The closer should be followed by a comma. Then write your\nsignature below the closer, and if you have time, print your name below your signature.\n\nSample Conclusions\n\n                Case Notes                                       Conclusion                                    Analysis\n\n Plan                                          In view of the above signs and symptoms,\n Review BP, smoking reduction in 2             I believe he needs further investigations        Uses information from the writing\n months                                        including a prostate biopsy and surgical         task to formulate conclusion\n Refer to urologist – possible biopsy          management. I would appreciate your              Refers back to what was said in\n prostate                                      urgent attention for his condition.\n                                                                                                the body of the letter: In view of\n Writing Task                                  Yours sincerely,                                 the above signs and symptoms\n Write a referral letter addressed to Dr.                                                       Contains a polite request and\n David Booker (Urologist), 259 Wickham         Dr.X                                             maintains polite tone through the\n Tce, Brisbane 4001. Asl to be informed\n of the outcome.                                                                                use of modal verb: would\n                                                                                                Includes suspected diagnosis and\n In your answer:                                                                                level of urgency\n\n * Expand the relevant case notes into\n complete sentences\n * Do not use note form\n * The body of the letter should not be\n more than 200 words\n * Use correct letter format\n\n Assessment                                    Based on my provisional diagnosis of a\n Bilateral inguinal hernia                     bilateral inguinal hernia, I would like to       Uses information from the ﬁnal\n Advised patient you want to refer him to      refer him for surgery as early as possible.      consultation and writing task to\n a surgeon. He agreed but says he wants        Please note, that Mr Warden wishes to            formulate conclusion\n a local anaesthetic as a friend advised       have the surgery under local anaesthesia.\n him he will have less after effects than                                                       Uses sophisticated vocabulary\n with general anaesthetic.                     Yours sincerely,                                 and expression: Based on my\n Writing Task                                                                                   provisional diagnosis of\n Write a letter addressed to Dr. Glynn         Dr X (GP)                                        Adds extra information speciﬁc to\n Howard, 249 Wickham Tce, Brisbane,\n 4001 explaining the patient's current                                                          the task with the expression:\n condition.                                                                                     Please note,\n\n\n Plan                                          I believe he needs cardiovascular\n Suspected angina - refer to cardiologist      investigations in order to rule out angina       Uses information from the writing\n for cardiovascular assessment.                pectoris. I would appreciate it if you could     task to formulate conclusion\n Writing Task                                  see him as early as possible and advise          Maintains polite tone through the\n Write a referral letter to cardiologist Dr.   him on further management.\n Ken Wilson. Suite 5, Greenslopes                                                               use of modal verbs would & could\n Hospital Medical Centre, Brisbane 4121.       Yours sincerely                                  Uses appropriate expression: in\n                                                                                                order to rule out\n                                               Dr Z\n\f\nAssessment                                  I believe that Catherine needs an urgent\nDepression. Severe. ?Bi polar               psychiatric consultation regarding her           Summarises chief complaint in\nNeeds urgent treatment                      acute episode of depression and I would          original language\nCalled to husband                           appreciate it if you could take over her         Maintains polite tone through the\nWriting Task                                care for appropriate treatment.\nWrite a referral letter to psychiatrist                                                      use of modal verbs would & could\nDr. Abe Normal Brampston St, Mt                                                              Uses polite request for on going\nGravatt ,4121,QLD.                          Yours sincerely,                                 care\n\n                                            Dr X (GP)\n\nAssessment                                  I would appreciate your assessment and\nDiagnosed peritonitis with perforation      emergency management of this patient’s           Note, sometimes a brief\nWriting Task                                condition. If you need further information,      conclusion is all that is required....\nRefer urgently to the Emergency             please feel free to contact me.                  or all that you will have time for!\nDepartment Admitting Doctor\n                                            Yours sincerely,                                 Although it is a memorised phrase\n                                                                                             to some degree, it is\n                                            Dr X                                             grammatically correct, concise,\n                                                                                             direct and contains a level of\n                                                                                             urgency\n                                                                                             Maintains polite tone through the\n                                                                                             use of modal verb would\n                                                                                             Contains an offer of future\n                                                                                             support\n\n\n\n\n                                                        Common Errors\n                            Incorrect                                                         Correct\n\n    I would very much appreciated your attention regarding             I would very much appreciate your attention regarding\n    further management of Mr. Henderson.                               further management of Mr. Henderson.(active)\n                                                                       Your attention regarding further management of Mr.\nExplanation: Incorrect grammar, see above\n                                                                       Henderson would be very much appreciated.(passive)\n\n\n\n    If you have any query, please do not hesitate to contact           If you have any queries, please do not hesitate to\n    me.                                                                contact me.\n\nExplanation: Use plural form of query\n\n\n    I will appreciate your further assessment and                      I would appreciate your further assessment and\n    management                                                         management.\n    In view of the above ﬁndings I believe he needs an                 In view of the above ﬁndings I believe he needs an\n    abdominal CT scan. Therefore, I will greatly appreciate            abdominal CT scan. Therefore, I would greatly\n    your further assessment for Mr Backo.                              appreciate your further assessment for Mr Backo.\n\nExplanation: Polite form \"would\" required instead of would\n\f\nKindly investigate this child and do the needful. If you          I would appreciate it if you could investigate this child’s\n     need any more information regarding her situation,                condition and do the necessary management. If you\n     please try to contact me without any hesitation.                  require anymore information, please do not hesitate to\n                                                                       contact me.\n Explanation: Several errors here. Basically it is important that\n the standard patterns and style conventions are followed in\n conclusions.\n\n\n     Based on above history and physical ﬁndings, I suspect\n     she may have a ruptured rupt. I will appreciate if you            Based on the above history and physical ﬁndings, I\n     would offer your expert assessment to this lady. Please           suspect she may have a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. I\n     keep me informed of the outcome.                                  would appreciate it if you could offer your expert\n Explanation: Two errors: 1. Incorrect use of modal 2. omission        assessment to this lady. Please keep me informed of\n of the deﬁnite article the                                            the outcome.\n\n\n\n\n I would be appreciated if you could take over the care of this\n patient.                                                              It would be appreciated if you could take over the care\n Explanation: Incorrect grammar                                        of this patient. (passive verb)\n                                                                       I would be appreciative if you could take over the care of\n                                                                       this patient.(be + adjective)\n                                                                       I would appreciate it if you could take over the care of\n                                                                       this patient. (active verb)\n\n\n\n   wink Handy Tip\nAs with introductions, when writing conclusions, ﬁnd a style and pattern which you are conﬁdent with and use it. However, take\ncare to understand the basic grammar rules and always remember to respond to the task question. Practice writing conclusions\nusing the sample case notes provided in your course.\n\n\n\n\nLast modiﬁed: Sunday, 11 February 2018, 12:26 PM\n\n\n                                                        Return to: Topic 1 \n\f\nDoctors: Writing Course\n\n\n\n                                                                 Dos & Don’ts\n                                       Below are a list of simple points to remember on the day of your exam.\n\n                                    Do                                                                    Don't\n Summarise all the information from the case notes into sections         Follow a strict chronological order as your letter may become too\n such as: treatment given and obvious trends, medication, medical        long, difﬁcult to read and will not focus on the main problem and\n history. This will be both easier to write and read as well as          related factors.\n avoiding repition\n\n Try to write somewhere between 180 and 200 words for the body           Write over 220 words as it will effect your overall result. You being\n of the letter. This is the requirement of OET and the assessors are     tested on your ability to write a clear concise letter, not a long\n quite strict in this area.                                              letter.\n\n                                                                         Don’t write under 160 words as there may not be sufﬁcient\n                                                                         language to get a B grade.\n\n Omit information which is not directly relevant to your task. This is   Try to put all the information from the case notes into the letter.\n a big trap for many candidates in that they try to write down all the   Your letter will be too long and also poorly organised and difﬁcult\n information from the task sheet. This does not reﬂect reality.          to read\n\n Expand on all acronyms. For example OPG should be written as            Overuse acronyms. You are being tested on you ability to expand\n orthopantamogram, BP as blood pressure and PR as pulse rate &           on case notes so make sure you do.\n hx as history\n\n Provide a simple clear summary of the condition so that a lay           Use too much medical jargon. Remember it is a test of English not\n person could understand                                                 Latin!\n\n Spend time reading the case notes and grouping information which        Start writing without planning your letter. You should allow 15\n are related such as medication, persistant high blood pressure etc      minutes reading case notes and planning the letter.\n etc\n\n\n\n Use synonyms so that you can express