  headaches. On examination, she was            Supporting sentences expand the\nVomited twice last night and headaches    severely febrile with a temperature of 40.2\nworse                                     and a pulse rate of 110 beats per minute.     case notes into complete\nObjective                                 There was macula-papular rash over the        sentences, note the use of verbs,\nFBC- WBC(18000) and left shift            legs and neck stiffness was present.          articles and conjunctions (and)\nUrinary Function Report Normal            Blood test showed leucocytosis with a         Signal word shows empathy\nTemperature 40.2C                         shift to the left..\nPulse 110/min                                                                               Regrettably,\nMacula-papular rash over legs\nNeck Stiffness+                                                                             On examination,\n\n\n\n\nExample 2\n\n            Case Notes                                  Paragraphs                                   Analysis\nMedical History                           Initially, she presented to me on 21/2/10,\nThyroidism diagnosed Feb 07               complaining of inﬂamed, sticky and            The topic sentence is begins with\nHigh blood pressure June 09               weeping eyes. Both her eyes were reddish      the ﬁrst consultation\nHip replacement July 09                   with watery discharge. However, her right     Supporting sentences transform\nMedications – thyroxine 1mg daily,        eye was worse than the left eye.\nAtacand 4mg daily, Fosamax 10mg daily     Therefore, she was prescribed chlorisig       case notes into complete\nNo known allergies                        drops 4 hourly. In terms of her medical       sentences\n21.02.10                                  history, she has had thyroidism for 3         Includes both medical history and\nSubjective                                years, high blood pressure for 1 year and     initial consultation\nComplains of inﬂamed, sticky and          a hip replacement was done in 2005. Her\nweeping eyes.                             current medications are Thyroxin 1 mg,        Signal words express a time line,\nObjective                                 Atacand 4 mg and Fosamax 10 mg daily.         contrast and cause and effect.\nBP 135 /75 P 74                           She has no known allergies.\nBoth eyes – red, watery discharge right                                                     Initially,\neye worse than left                                                                         However,\n                                                                                            Therefore,....\n                                                                                            In terms of...\n                                                                                            ..current\n\n\n03.03.10                                  On review 2 weeks later, she had made no\nSubjective                                improvement. In addition, she had blurred     Topic sentence is introduced with\nNo improvement to eyes, blurred vision    vision with odematous eye lids and            the phrase: On review 2 weeks later\nObjective                                 conjunctival conjestion.Therefore,            Explains medication details clearly\nOdema eye lids ++                         chloramphenicol was prescribed 0.5% one\nMarked conjunctival congestion            drop three times daily and Bion tears one     in complete sentences\nPlan                                      drop 4 hourly. A review was schedule after    Signal words continue time line\nChloramphenicol 0.5% sterile 1 drop TID   2 weeks.\nBion Tears 1 drop each eye 4 hrly                                                           Two weeks later,\nReview 2 weeks                                                                              In addition,\n                                                                                            Therefore,", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 664, "text": "05.06.10                                    Unfortunately, today she was\n Subjective                                  accompanied by her husband with                   Summarises medical condition\n Accompanied by husband. Very                complaints of impaired vision in both             clearly and concisely with the\n distressed. Has lost most sight in both     eyes and an inability to read books or            expression \"vision Impairment\"\n eyes –can make out light or dark shapes     watch television. There was oedema in\n but unable to read or watch TV.             both eyelids with white discharge. She            Supporting sentences expand\n Objective                                   could not read the eye                            case notes into formal sentences\n Marked oedema upper and lower lids                                                            Signal words add sympathy\n White sticky discharge Unable to read\n eye chart                                                                                         Unfortunately,\n Plan\n Refer immediately Emergency Dept,\n Royal Melbourne Eye Hospital.\n Husband will drive to hospital\n\n\n\n\n   wink Handy Hint\nWhen describing the history, base your letter from today’s perspective. That means, include all the relevant past history, but in\nsummary form, eliminating less important detail and focus your attention on the current situation which is usually more\nimportant.\n\n\n\n\nLast modiﬁed: Sunday, 11 February 2018, 12:23 PM\n\n\n                                                       Return to: Topic 1 ", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 665, "text": "Doctors: Writing Course\n\n\n\n                                                         Conclusions\n\nThe conclusion or ﬁnal paragraph in the letter should be fairly standard in structure. It should be based on the task question which\nis found at the end of case notes. It may contain one or two of the following points:\n\n    a polite request of action required\n    a summary of your (suspected) diagnosis\n    a thank you for ongoing support\n    an offer of future assistance if required (this can be useful if you choose to omit some details from the case notes)\n\nIt is useful to be familiar with some standard patterns so that you are able to conclude your letter conﬁdently, quickly and most\nimportantly, accurately. However, some degree of originality will impress the assessors. Therefore, where possible try to ensure\nthat your conclusion is related to your task and not simply a memorised ending.\n     wink Handy hint\nWhen describing the chief complaint or your diagnosis, try to use different words or sentence structure to what you have said in\nthe introduction.\n\n\n                                                 Important Grammar Rules\n\nModal Verbs : Some modal verbs & the verb hope are used to convey politeness and commonly used for polite requests in the\nconclusion of formal letters. Compare the patterns below.\n\n                 Informal                                     Polite                                    More polite", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 666, "text": "I want you to see him as early as           Please see him as early as                   I would appreciate it if you could\n     possible and advise him on                  possible and advise him on further           see him as early as possible and\n     further management.                         management.(this sounds like an              advise him on further\n     I will be pleased if you can                order so should not be used)                 management.\n     examine, diagnose and treat the             Please examine, diagnose and                 It would be greatly appreciated if\n     patient as you feel appropriate.            treat the patient as you feel                you could see him as early as\n     Can you take over her care for              appropriate.                                 possible and advise him on further\n     appropriate treatment.                      Please take over her care for                management. (passive and most\n                                                 appropriate treatment.                       polite)\n                                                                                              I would be grateful if you could\n                                                                                              examine, diagnose and treat the\n                                                                                              patient as you feel appropriate.\n                                                                                              (active)\n Explanation: These sentences are ﬁne in                                                      I would appreciate it if you could\n spoken English, but not suitable for                                                         examine, diagnose and treat the\n formal writing. Can and will are            Explanation: These sentences are                 patient as you feel appropriate.\n considered less polite than could and       acceptable in written English, but can           (active)\n would.                                      sound quite direct.                              I would appreciate it if you could\n                                                                                              take over her care for appropriate\n                                                                                              treatment.\n                                                                                              I would be grateful if you could\n                                                                                              take over her care for appropriate\n                                                                                              treatment.\n\n                                                                                          Explanation: These sentences are the\n                                                                                          most suitable for concluding requests in\n                                                                                          formal letters.\n\n\nConditional Sentences: These sentences are also frequently used in the conclusion of a referral letter and the rules are as follows:\n\n   Use a comma when the if clause is at the beginning of the          Don’t use a comma when the if clause is at the end of the\n                        sentence.                                                          sentence.\n\n\n     If you could take over her on going care,it would be               It would be greatly appreciated if you could take over\n     greatly appreciated.                                               her ongoing care.\n     If you have any further questions regarding this patient,          Please don’t hesitate to call me if you have any further\n     please don’t hesitate to call me.                                  questions regarding this patient.\n\n\n\n     If you require any more information, please don't                  Please don't hesitate to contact me if you require any\n     hesitate to contact me.(active)                                    more information.(active)\n     If any more information is required, please don't hesitate         Please don't hesitate to contact me if any more\n     to contact me.(passive)                                            information is required.(passive)", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 667, "text": "Should you have any further queries, please don't                     Please don't hesitate to contact me should you have any\n     hesitate to contact me.                                               further queries.\n\n Note: Sometimes if is omitted from a conditional sentence. In\n full the sentence means:\n If you should have any further questions regarding this patient,\n please don’t hesitate to call me.\n\n\nCloser and signature\nLeave a space between the last line of the conclusion and the closer. The closer should be followed by a comma. Then write your\nsignature below the closer, and if you have time, print your name below your signature.\n\nSample Conclusions\n\n                Case Notes                                       Conclusion                                    Analysis\n\n Plan                                          In view of the above signs and symptoms,\n Review BP, smoking reduction in 2             I believe he needs further investigations        Uses information from the writing\n months                                        including a prostate biopsy and surgical         task to formulate conclusion\n Refer to urologist – possible biopsy          management. I would appreciate your              Refers back to what was said in\n prostate                                      urgent attention for his condition.\n                                                                                                the body of the letter: In view of\n Writing Task                                  Yours sincerely,                                 the above signs and symptoms\n Write a referral letter addressed to Dr.                                                       Contains a polite request and\n David Booker (Urologist), 259 Wickham         Dr.X                                             maintains polite tone through the\n Tce, Brisbane 4001. Asl to be informed\n of the outcome.                                                                                use of modal verb: would\n                                                                                                Includes suspect